ually, for the earlier period 1946-1949: $4.03 = £1, so $1 = £0.2481\n\n# Actually, let's use the official IMF par values:\n# 1944-1946: $4.03/£ (official)\n# 1946-1949: $4.03/£ \n# 1949-1967: $2.80/£ \n# 1967-1971: $2.40/£\n\n# The fiscal year 1948 would be Jul 1947 - Jun 1948, which uses $4.03 rate\n# Fiscal year 1949 would be Jul 1948 - Jun 1949, uses $4.03 for most, $2.80 after Sept 1949\n# But in practice, let me use historical annual averages\n\n# From historical records:\nrates_by_year = {\n    1948: 0.2481,  # £ per $1 (pre-devaluation)\n    1949: 0.2481,  # Most of the year (devaluation in Sept)\n    1950: 0.3571,  # Post-devaluation\n    1951: 0.3571,\n    1952: 0.3571,\n    1953: 0.3571,\n    1954: 0.3571,\n    1955: 0.3571,\n    1956: 0.3571,\n    1957: 0.3571,\n    1958: 0.3571,\n    1959: 0.3571,\n    1960: 0.3571,\n    1961: 0.3571,\n    1962: 0.3571,\n    1963: 0.3571,\n    1964: 0.3571,\n}\n\nfor year, rate in rates_by_year.items():\n    print(f\"{year}: ${1/rate:.4f} = £1 or $1 = £{rate:.4f}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
