t $4.03, could use average\n- Fiscal year 1950 onwards (July 1949 - June 1950+): At $2.80\n\nFor yearly average, I might use something closer to $3.50 for 1949 since it was devalued midway through. But given the constraints of the Bretton Woods system and the fixed nature of these rates, my calculations should be reasonably accurate. The final Pearson correlation coefficient of 0.98 seems correct given the data.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_9490445f4a7834a7","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"todo__todo_write","arguments":{"content":"- [x] Explore available treasury bulletin resources\n- [x] Extract Net Receipts and Expenditures data for fiscal years 1948-1964\n- [x] Find GBP/USD exchange rates for each year (1948-1964)\n- [x] Convert all figures from USD to GBP\n- [x] Calculate Pearson correlation coefficient\n- [ ] Write final answer to /app/answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"todo"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
